            "We have the equation:\n\n\\[\n1 + A = \\frac{B}{C}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n\n- \\( A' = 10.0584 \\)\n- \\( B' = 6.1053 \\)\n\nWe need to solve for \\( C \\). First, let's substitute the known values into the equation:\n\n\\[\n1 + 10.0584 = \\frac{6.1053}{C}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[\n11.0584 = \\frac{6.1053}{C}\n\\]\n\nNow, solve for \\( C \\) by rearranging the equation:\n\n\\[\nC = \\frac{6.1053}{11.0584}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the value:\n\n\\[\nC \\approx 0.5515\n\\]\n\nSo, the value of \\( C \\) is approximately **0.5515**."
